## 7. Database

The README **must** contain a concise representation of the actual
Supabase/PostgreSQL schema.

Before updating this section, inspect `supabase/migrations/`,
`docs/architecture/data-model.md`, and relevant technical
specifications. Generate the overview from the actual schema. Do not
guess table names or relationships. Do not paste complete SQL into the
README.

Provide a compact ER-style diagram when readable, and a compact table
when useful (table, purpose, key relationships). Only document actual
tables and relationships.

Link to the data-model doc, relevant TS files, and
`supabase/migrations/`.

**Database security:** briefly explain PostgreSQL, Supabase, Row Level
Security, and authorization/access-control behaviour **only if those
controls exist**.

## 8. Project structure

Show the important **real** repository structure. Inspect at least
`src/`, `supabase/migrations/`, `tests/`, `docs/`,
`.github/workflows/`, `.cursor/`.

Adjust the tree to match the repository. For each important directory:
what it contains and how a developer uses it. Do not list every file.
Do not invent directories.

Extending `src/` or adding SQL under `supabase/migrations/` must be
described as part of the approved development process. Do not imply
that developers should freely create application/database structure
outside documented planning gates (`PLANNED` + approved plan;
`docs/sdlc/lifecycle.md`).

## 9. Engineering and skills

Communicate what the project demonstrates. Organize by capability, not
a flat technology catalogue. Only include practices and tools
**supported by the repository**.

**Product / business analysis** (when supported): requirements,
functional specifications, user stories, acceptance criteria, business
rules, process analysis, translating requirements into implementation
and testing.

**QA and testing:** functional, regression, integration, E2E,
automated, negative testing, test design, defect analysis, quality
gates. Include API testing **only** if API/Newman/Postman (or
equivalent) actually exists.

**Data and database** (when supported): SQL, PostgreSQL, Supabase,
relational modelling, relationships, validation, RLS, test data.

**Development:** TypeScript, React, application architecture, Git,
code review, refactoring, automated validation.

**CI/CD:** GitHub Actions, automated quality checks, automated tests,
release workflows, production smoke testing — as actually configured.

## 10. AI-assisted engineering

Link `docs/ai/README.md` if it exists.

Explain:

- `.cursor/rules/` — always-on project constraints and instructions
- `.cursor/skills/` — on-demand procedures invoked when appropriate

Name skills that **actually exist on disk**. Do not claim that skills
execute automatically.

### What the agent does when asked

The agent may assist with: requirements/specification drafting; user
stories and AC; BDD; test design; implementation planning;
implementation **after** `PLANNED`; test creation; test execution when
`execute-tests` is explicitly invoked; code review support; pull-request
preparation; documentation updates; README maintenance when this rule
requires it.

Skills do **not** start by themselves. Do not describe the agent as a
background, scheduled, or autonomous workflow.

### What the human must do

Preserve human decision gates. The human is responsible for:

- Definition of Ready
- approving the implementation plan / setting `PLANNED`
- QA validation on `test`
- merging to `main`
- adding GitHub secret `PRODUCTION_SUPABASE_DB_URL` once
- approving Vercel production deployment
- explicitly invoking relevant skills/prompts

The agent must **never** merge `main`. Production schema application
is performed by the production smoke workflow, not by pasting SQL in
the Supabase Dashboard.

